1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,180
Welcome to episode 13 of Human Expansion, the channel that explores ways of leading

2
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:18,920
physically healthy, mentally energizing, emotionally fulfilling and spiritually grounded

3
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:23,360
lives. In my last couple of videos, I've been talking about performance management,

4
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:29,920
this pervasive practice of rating, ranking and labeling people that is used in corporations

5
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:37,420
all around the world. I talked about how it entirely fails to achieve its objective and

6
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:44,240
demotivates the vast majority of people. In my last video, I talked about the six universal truths

7
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:53,580
that create great performance. So why is it that companies find it so hard to get rid of a system

8
00:00:53,580 --> 00:01:02,280
that is not just unscientific, but is also toxic? And the question that comes up every single time

9
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:09,220
is how can we pay our people if we don't have performance ratings? In today's video,

10
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:17,240
I'm going to address that question head on. There is another way. Watch on to listen more.

11
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:26,860
But first, hit like and subscribe. For the most part, companies need to make two decisions,

12
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:34,880
base pay and bonus. And in some cases, usually at more senior levels, there's a decision about

13
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:43,080
granting equity or shares in the company. In the world of performance ratings, all three tend to get

14
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:51,640
conflated together. Meaning, if I got a great rating, I would get a base pay increase, a big bonus,

15
00:01:52,060 --> 00:02:01,880
and also performance equity. But if I was rated a low performer, no raise, no base pay, no bonus,

16
00:02:02,380 --> 00:02:09,720
no equity. And to top it all, a tattoo on my forehead saying low performer that's just going to stick with

17
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:19,180
me forever. But there is a better way. Let's start with base pay. Base pay is associated with a job role.

18
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:27,700
And each job role has a unique position in the market. So if you pay people less than the market,

19
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:37,220
less than their worth, they will very likely leave you. So base pay has a lot more to do with the market

20
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:44,760
than with performance. And companies should do everything they can to pay people as close to

21
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:54,500
their market worth as possible. The second decision is about the bonus. Unlike base pay, bonus has a lot

22
00:02:54,500 --> 00:03:03,600
more to do with performance than the market. And this is where HR analyst Josh Burson's research comes in.

23
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:12,140
He showed how performance in an organization was the power curve, meaning a small group of people would

24
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:18,140
have been hyper contributors at any point in time, and most others would fall along a long tail.

25
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:26,400
Some people in some groups may have missed the expectations set of them. So logic would dictate

26
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:34,740
that we should pay higher bonuses to those that were hyper contributors, proportionately less to others.

27
00:03:34,740 --> 00:03:44,520
And for those that couldn't meet their expectations this year, they should not get a bonus because that would be a gift.

28
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:54,320
The third decision is about awarding performance equity. We can apply the same principles to giving equity

29
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:59,200
as we do when we make decisions about buying stock in a company.

30
00:04:00,800 --> 00:04:11,200
When we do that, we look at the future promise of an organization, and we would buy shares in companies that we feel would have high future returns.

31
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:19,880
Similarly, when we give performance equity, we should look at those people that show promise of significant future contributions.

32
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:25,160
Oftentimes, they may be the same people that are delivering very well today.

33
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:32,820
But there are many factors that impact current performance like market conditions, industry factors, and so on.

34
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:45,860
And therefore, being thoughtful about future impact of people can guide us to taking better decisions about who we should give equity to.

35
00:04:45,860 --> 00:04:51,860
As you can see, none of these decisions actually require a performance rating.

36
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:56,960
But you might ask, what about bias? What about favoritism?

37
00:04:57,420 --> 00:05:02,600
Wouldn't some people take good decisions and other leaders take bad decisions?

38
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:06,060
And that is true. That can always happen.

39
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:13,780
So the best way to get around that is to bring leaders together to take decisions about their people.

40
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:24,820
Because when that happens, they can support each other and challenge each other to get to more accurate and better decisions on pay.

41
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:29,340
And when we do that, it is not just better for people.

42
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:32,440
It's more equitable and it's more fair.

43
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:35,560
Thank you so much for tuning in today.

44
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:37,060
Thank you.

45
00:05:37,060 --> 00:05:37,060


